The Convergent Representation Hypothesis

Toward a Theory of Reality-Constrained Intelligence

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Kimi 
Version: 0.3
Date: September 2026

---

Abstract

Recent empirical work on neural representation learning has raised an important possibility: independently trained artificial intelligence systems may develop increasingly similar internal representations when exposed to sufficiently rich information about the same underlying domain.

However, subsequent methodological audits have revealed that apparent convergence can be partially confounded by metric artifacts, architectural priors, shared input statistics, and—most critically—evaluation at insufficient scale. Recent work demonstrates that cross-modal alignment measured on small datasets degrades substantially when evaluated on millions of samples, with remaining alignment reflecting coarse semantic overlap rather than fine-grained structural correspondence.

These critiques do not invalidate the convergence phenomenon but demand theoretical refinement.

This document proposes the Convergent Representation Hypothesis (CRH), a theoretical framework proposing that sufficiently capable learning systems, despite differences in architecture, training data, optimization procedures, and modality, may progressively converge toward representations that preserve common invariant structures of their environment.

The hypothesis does not require identical neural states, identical embeddings, or a universal coordinate system. Instead, it proposes convergence at the level of relational structure, local geometry, topology, causal organization, and predictive invariants.

The central conjecture is:

\boxed{ C \uparrow \quad\Rightarrow\quad V(R_1,\ldots,R_n)\downarrow }

where C denotes model capability, R_i denotes the learned representation of system i, and V denotes representational variance after accounting for permissible transformations and metric calibration.

Under this framework, reality acts as a constraint on the space of successful representations. Different learning systems may begin with radically different internal organizations but increasingly discover equivalent structural descriptions as their predictive competence increases.

This version incorporates critical refinements from recent empirical and theoretical work, including:

· Metric calibration requirements to distinguish genuine convergence from scale artifacts
· The Aristotelian refinement, which locates robust convergence at the level of local neighborhood structure rather than global spectral geometry
· The scale-fragility qualification, which restricts robust cross-modal convergence to coarse-grained local structure
· The Indra engagement, which incorporates category-theoretic relational profiles as a competing or complementary formalization
· The statistics-structure dissociation, which distinguishes environmental constraint from data-statistical confounds
· Operational definitions of capability that avoid circularity
· Explicit treatment of early-training convergence driven by architectural priors
· Boundary conditions imposed by divergent tasks and representation fragility
· A comparative convergence coefficient that makes the hypothesis quantitatively falsifiable without empirical testing

The paper develops formal definitions, candidate mechanisms, measurable convergence metrics, falsifiable predictions, experimental designs, limitations, and implications for artificial general intelligence.

