1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence systems are constructed using radically different engineering choices. Different architectures employ different mechanisms of computation. Different systems are trained on different datasets. Different laboratories use different optimization strategies, objectives, modalities, and forms of supervision.

One might therefore expect their internal representations to remain fundamentally idiosyncratic.

Yet a growing body of representation-learning research suggests that independently trained models can exhibit substantial similarities in the structures they learn.

This raises a deeper possibility.

Perhaps intelligence is not completely free to construct arbitrary internal models of the world. Perhaps the structure of the world constrains the structure of successful intelligence.

1.1 From Platonic to Aristotelian Convergence

The original Platonic Representation Hypothesis proposed that neural networks converge toward a shared statistical model of reality. Subsequent work has substantially refined this picture.

Gröger, Wen & Brbić demonstrated that global spectral similarity metrics—such as Centered Kernel Alignment—are systematically confounded by network scale. Increasing model depth or width can inflate similarity scores even when representation spaces are unrelated. After applying permutation-based null calibration, apparent global convergence largely disappears, while local neighborhood similarity retains significant cross-model agreement.

This has led to what they term the Aristotelian Representation Hypothesis: models converge to shared local neighborhood relationships, not to a global statistical model of reality.

CRH incorporates this refinement directly. The robust convergence phenomenon is local-structural, while global convergence remains a stronger, contingent claim requiring specific conditions.

1.2 The Scale-Fragility Refinement

A further critical development emerged in 2026. A large-scale empirical audit demonstrated that cross-modal alignment degrades substantially when evaluated at scale—from approximately 1,000 samples to millions of samples. The alignment that remains between model representations reflects coarse semantic overlap rather than consistent fine-grained structure. Furthermore, the one-to-one image-caption constraint used in prior evaluations breaks down in realistic many-to-many settings.

This does not falsify convergence claims, but it demands a qualification: cross-modal convergence, if it exists, may be restricted to coarse categorical structure and may not extend to fine-grained relational topology. CRH therefore revises its cross-modal predictions to predict coarse-grained local convergence, with fine-grained convergence remaining a contingent claim requiring specific conditions.

1.3 The Indra Alternative

A competing theoretical framework, the Indra Representation Hypothesis, formalizes convergent representations not as isolated embeddings but as relational profiles—each entity defined by its cost-weighted relationships to all others—using the \mathcal{V}-enriched Yoneda embedding from category theory.

CRH and Indra are compatible at the philosophical level but differ in formalization: CRH emphasizes capability-dependent geometric convergence toward environmental structure, while Indra emphasizes the inherent relationality of any sufficiently trained representation. A unified perspective might view the Universal Representation Manifold as the Yoneda embedding of the environmental state category, with model-specific representations as approximate realizations of this relational profile.

1.4 The Distinction That Matters

Consider two hypothetical civilizations independently studying molecular physics.

Civilization A develops a graph-based mathematical formalism. Civilization B develops a completely different computational language.

Their symbols, coordinates, algorithms, and internal representations could be radically different.

Nevertheless, if both civilizations become sufficiently competent at predicting molecular behavior, they must eventually capture relationships corresponding to the same physical constraints.

They may describe these constraints differently. But they cannot escape them.

This suggests a distinction between representation identity and structural equivalence. The first is probably unnecessary. The second may be fundamental.

CRH proposes that structural equivalence is most robustly expressed at the level of local relational topology—how entities relate to their neighbors in representation space—rather than global coordinate geometry.

