4. Reality as a Constraint

The central theoretical mechanism is simple. The environment contains structure.

Let S \in \mathcal{S} denote the latent state of an environment. Observations are generated through:

x = g(S)

A learning system receives observations x and attempts to predict consequences:

\hat{y} = f(x)

If the prediction problem is sufficiently rich, successful prediction requires recovering information about the underlying state S.

Consequently, the set of representations capable of achieving high predictive performance may become increasingly constrained.

Define \mathcal{R}^*(C) as the set of representations capable of supporting capability level C.

The hypothesis predicts that C_1 < C_2 implies:

\mathcal{R}^*(C_2) \subseteq \mathcal{R}^*(C_1)

In words: higher capability may correspond to a narrower region of representational possibilities.

This provides a theoretical explanation for convergence. The models are not necessarily communicating with one another. They may simply be increasingly constrained by the same world.

4.1 Early Crystallization

Kapoor et al. conducted a large-scale audit of convergent learning and found that nearly all eventual alignment crystallizes within the first epoch—well before accuracy plateaus—indicating it is largely driven by shared input statistics and architectural biases, not by the final task solution.

This challenges the causal chain:

\text{Scale} \rightarrow \text{Capacity} \rightarrow \text{Deeper Structure} \rightarrow \text{Constraint} \rightarrow \text{Convergence}

If convergence is locked in by architectural priors and input statistics early in training, then the "ability to model deeper structure" may not be the operative mechanism.

CRH v0.3 addresses this by proposing two distinct convergence mechanisms:

Mechanism 1 (Early-Stage / Prior-Driven): Convergence arising from shared architectural inductive biases and shared input statistics. This mechanism operates early in training and may dominate observed similarity in standard experimental setups.

Mechanism 2 (Late-Stage / Capability-Driven): Convergence arising from the constraints imposed by environmental structure as models achieve sufficient predictive competence. This mechanism operates late in training and is the primary theoretical target of CRH.

The central conjecture concerns Mechanism 2. However, empirical tests must distinguish Mechanism 1 from Mechanism 2, which requires measuring alignment at initialization, after early training, and at convergence, as well as controlling for architectural and input-statistical confounds.

4.2 The Scale-Fragility of Cross-Modal Convergence

A recent large-scale empirical audit challenges the robustness of cross-modal convergence. When evaluated on millions rather than thousands of samples, mutual-nearest-neighbor alignment between text, image, audio, and video representations degrades substantially. The remaining alignment reflects coarse semantic overlap rather than consistent fine-grained structure. Furthermore, the one-to-one caption-image constraint used in prior evaluations breaks down in realistic many-to-many settings.

This does not falsify CRH, but it demands a qualification: cross-modal convergence, if it exists, may be restricted to coarse categorical structure and may not extend to fine-grained relational topology. CRH therefore revises Prediction 4 to predict coarse-grained local convergence across modalities, with fine-grained convergence remaining a contingent claim requiring specific conditions (e.g., shared task structure or explicit alignment objectives).

4.3 Data Statistics as a Confound

Even when training datasets are independently constructed, they may share statistical symmetries—power-law distributions, hierarchical structure, compositional sparsity—that independently shape representation geometry. This suggests that apparent convergence may be driven by statistics rather than structure.

CRH must therefore develop a formal theory of how to distinguish:

· Convergence driven by shared environmental structure (CRH's claim)
· Convergence driven by shared statistical regularities in data (a confound)
· Convergence driven by architectural inductive biases (a confound)
· Convergence driven by task optimization geometry (a confound)

The statistics-structure dissociation experiment described in Section 3.3 provides a direct test.

