5. Representation Versus Coordinate System

A major methodological problem is that neural representations possess substantial coordinate freedom.

Suppose R_A(x) = z. Another model might represent the same information as R_B(x) = Az + b, where A is an invertible transformation.

Raw vector comparison would incorrectly conclude that the models have very different representations.

Therefore CRH does not predict convergence of arbitrary coordinates. It predicts convergence of properties invariant under permissible transformations.

5.1 The Metric Calibration Imperative

A second, equally serious problem is that similarity metrics themselves can produce artifacts.

Recent work demonstrates that global spectral similarity measures are systematically confounded by network scale. After applying permutation-based null calibration, apparent global convergence largely disappears, while local neighborhood similarity retains significant cross-modal agreement.

CRH therefore requires that all convergence claims be tested using calibrated metrics that control for network width and depth, architectural inductive biases, input statistics, and random initialization effects. Only convergence that survives such calibration can be attributed to environmental constraint rather than measurement artifact.

5.2 Candidate Invariants

Candidate structural invariants include:

· Local neighborhood relationships (most robust, Aristotelian)
· Coarse categorical structure (robust at scale)
· Fine-grained instance structure (less robust, degrades at scale)
· Pairwise distances (less robust, scale-sensitive)
· Topology (persistent homology features)
· Causal relations (requires interventional testing)
· Compositional structure (requires systematicity testing)
· Symmetry (equivariance properties)
· Conservation relationships (dynamic invariants)
· Predictive equivalence (functional correspondence)
· Relational profiles (Yoneda embedding, Indra perspective)

CRH v0.3 predicts that coarse local neighborhood relationships are the most robust convergence target, while fine-grained structure, global distance structure, and spectral alignment are more fragile and may not survive calibration or scale.

The question is not "Do the neurons look the same?" The question is: "Do the models construct equivalent mathematical relationships among the things they represent—at least at the level of coarse local structure?"

