6. A Geometric Formulation

Let each model construct a representation manifold \mathcal{M}_i. The models may possess different coordinate systems.

Define an equivalence relation \mathcal{M}_i \sim \mathcal{M}_j if there exists an admissible transformation T_{ij} such that:

d_{\mathcal{M}_i}(x,y) \approx d_{\mathcal{M}_j}(T_{ij}(x), T_{ij}(y))

over a sufficiently large set of observations.

The degree of convergence can then be represented by:

\Gamma_{ij} = 1 - D(\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j)

where D is a transformation-aware, calibration-adjusted representational distance.

6.1 The Gromov-Wasserstein Formulation

For structural convergence claims, CRH recommends the Gromov-Wasserstein distance, which measures distance between metric measure spaces without requiring alignment of underlying coordinates:

d_{GW}\left( (\mathcal{M}_i, d_i, \mu_i), (\mathcal{M}_j, d_j, \mu_j) \right)

where d_i is the Riemannian metric on representation manifold \mathcal{M}_i, and \mu_i is the push-forward probability measure of environmental observations.

The central prediction becomes:

\lim_{C \to \infty} d_{GW}\left( (\mathcal{M}_i, d_i, \mu_i), (\mathcal{U}, d_{\mathcal{U}}, \mu_{\mathcal{U}}) \right) = 0

where \mathcal{U} is a hypothesized Universal Representation Manifold.

6.2 The Universal Manifold as a Relational Category

CRH v0.3 refines the concept of \mathcal{U} in light of the Indra framework. Rather than defining \mathcal{U} as a quotient manifold over environmental state space modulo unobservable variations—a construction that presumes a clean separation between observable and unobservable structure—CRH now defines \mathcal{U} as a category of relational profiles.

Under this definition, the universal object is not a geometric manifold but a functor assigning to each entity its complete relational profile: how it relates to all other entities under the environmental cost function. This is precisely the \mathcal{V}-enriched Yoneda embedding.

Model-specific representations approximate this universal relational category with varying fidelity. Convergence, in this view, is not movement toward a common geometric configuration, but improvement in the accuracy with which each model's internal representations capture the environmental relational structure.

This refinement addresses the concern that the quotient manifold construction requires a well-defined group of unobservable variations—an assumption that may not hold in general. The relational category formulation requires only that environmental entities stand in well-defined relationships to one another, which is the minimal assumption needed for the convergence hypothesis to be meaningful.

6.3 The Comparative Convergence Coefficient

To make CRH quantitatively falsifiable without empirical testing, we introduce the Comparative Convergence Coefficient \kappa.

Define:

\kappa(C_1, C_2) = \frac{\Gamma(C_2) - \Gamma(C_1)}{C_2 - C_1}

where \Gamma(C) is the calibrated, null-adjusted population-level convergence at capability level C, and C_1 < C_2.

CRH predicts:

\kappa > 0

That is, convergence should increase with capability.

The null hypothesis is:

\kappa = 0

That is, convergence is capability-independent, suggesting it is driven entirely by architectural priors and input statistics (Mechanism 1).

The alternative explanation—that convergence is driven by task optimization geometry rather than environmental structure (the contravariance hypothesis)—predicts:

\kappa_{\text{real}} = \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}

where \kappa_{\text{real}} is the coefficient for models trained on real physics simulation and \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} is the coefficient for models trained on pseudo-physics of identical computational complexity.

CRH predicts:

\kappa_{\text{real}} > \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}

This coefficient makes the theory falsifiable: if \kappa is zero or negative, CRH is wrong. If \kappa is positive but equal across real and pseudo-physics environments, the convergence is driven by optimization geometry, not environmental structure.

