7. The Role of Scale and Capability

The popular interpretation of the Platonic Representation Hypothesis emphasizes scale.

CRH proposes a more cautious version. Scale itself may not cause convergence.

Instead:

\text{Scale} \rightarrow \text{Capacity} \rightarrow \text{Ability to model deeper structure} \rightarrow \text{Representational constraint} \rightarrow \text{Potential convergence}

This distinction matters. A larger model can merely become a larger memorization machine.

7.1 Operationalizing Capability

CRH v0.3 operationalizes capability C as a composite index:

C = C(P, D, O, T)

where:

· P = parameter capacity (but only insofar as it enables functional capability)
· D = effective data coverage (out-of-distribution generalization, not merely training set size)
· O = objective quality (alignment with environmental invariants, not merely training loss)
· T = training regime (including curriculum, regularization, and optimization)

Specifically, C should be measured by:

1. Predictive accuracy on held-out probes targeting specific environmental invariants
2. Out-of-distribution generalization gap (smaller gap = higher capability)
3. Compositional generalization performance (systematic recombination of known primitives)
4. Causal intervention consistency (predictions under interventions match environmental structure)

Only when convergence is correlated with these functional capability metrics—not merely with scale—can CRH claim that reality-constraint is the operative mechanism.

7.2 A Critical Concern: Capability Circularity

A potential tautology threatens the framework: if capability is defined partly in terms of predictive accuracy, and if predictive accuracy requires recovering environmental structure, then the hypothesis may be analytically true—higher capability by definition involves recovering more environmental structure.

CRH v0.3 acknowledges this concern and responds by distinguishing:

· Analytic claim: High-capability systems must recover environmental information. This is true by definition and uninteresting.
· Empirical claim: High-capability systems converge on similar organizations of that recovered information. This is the substantive claim and requires empirical testing.

The interesting question is not whether high-capability systems share structure—they must—but how much structure they must share, what kind of structure is shared versus idiosyncratic, and whether this shared structure corresponds to environmental invariants or to confounds like architectural priors and data statistics.

