8. Scientific Domains as the Strongest Test

Scientific domains provide an unusually powerful experimental environment.

Consider molecular structures, protein folding, materials properties, physical simulations, and chemical reactions.

These domains contain relationships that are not arbitrary inventions of language or culture. For example:

\text{molecular structure} \rightarrow \text{energy} \rightarrow \text{stability} \rightarrow \text{function}

If independent models discover similar internal organization while predicting these properties, the probability that the similarity results purely from shared human semantics becomes smaller.

This makes scientific representation convergence an important testbed for CRH.

8.1 A Note on Realism

CRH implicitly assumes a realist position: there exists a structured reality that constrains successful representations. This assumption is foundational to the entire project and should be acknowledged explicitly.

An anti-realist might argue that "reality" itself is a construct—that our notion of molecular structure, energy, and stability are human inventions, not discoveries. From this perspective, convergence between AI systems might reflect convergence on human cognitive structures (because humans built the systems, the data, and the evaluation metrics), not on objective reality.

CRH does not resolve this philosophical dispute. It assumes realism and proceeds to investigate its empirical consequences. If the hypothesis is falsified, one possible explanation is that the realist assumption is wrong.

However, CRH v0.3 adds a critical qualification: the realism assumption is testable in a limited sense. If the statistics-structure dissociation experiment (Section 3.3) shows that convergence persists when statistics are altered but structure is preserved, and fails when structure is scrambled but statistics are preserved, this provides evidence that convergence is driven by environmental structure rather than data statistics. This does not resolve the metaphysical question, but it does demonstrate that the empirical phenomenon is not purely a product of human data-generation conventions.

8.2 The Construction Problem

Any environment we construct is itself a product of human design choices. A physics simulator encodes human-selected physical laws; a navigation task encodes human-selected goals; even a random environment encodes human choices about what to randomize. There is no "natural" environment that avoids human construction.

Furthermore, if agents are trained in completely artificial environments, convergence might reflect constraints of that particular environment rather than reality generally.

CRH v0.3 acknowledges this limitation explicitly: the leap from convergence in simulated environments to claims about reality-constrained intelligence requires theoretical justification that cannot be provided by experiments alone.

The strongest available response is triangulation: convergence claims are strengthened when they hold across multiple independently designed environments, multiple independently chosen observation channels, and multiple independently specified tasks. No single experiment can escape the construction problem, but convergence across diverse constructions provides cumulative evidence.

