9. Three Levels of Convergence

CRH distinguishes four increasingly strong forms of convergence, refined to incorporate the Aristotelian distinction, the scale-fragility critique, and the Indra relational perspective.

Level I: Statistical Convergence

Models exhibit similar distributions or correlations:

P(R_i) \approx P(R_j)

This is the weakest form and is most susceptible to metric artifacts.

Metric: Optimal Transport / Wasserstein Distance W_1(P(R_i), P(R_j))

Properties: Invariant to translation; sensitive to linear transforms.

Level IIa: Coarse Local-Topological Convergence (Robust Claim)

Models preserve similar coarse-grained local neighborhood relationships. For most observations x, the coarse category-level nearest neighbors of R_i(x) correspond to the coarse category-level nearest neighbors of R_j(x).

Metric: Calibrated k-NN graph Jaccard distance at coarse granularity; local CKA with null calibration

Properties: Invariant to non-linear transformations that preserve coarse local topology; robust to scale confounds; survives evaluation at scale.

Level IIb: Fine-Grained Local-Topological Convergence (Contingent Claim)

Models preserve similar fine-grained instance-level neighborhood relationships.

Metric: Calibrated k-NN graph Jaccard distance at fine granularity

Properties: More fragile than Level IIa; may degrade at scale; requires shared architectural priors, data distributions, or explicit alignment objectives.

Level IIc: Global-Geometric Convergence (Fragile Claim)

Models preserve similar global distance structure and spectral geometry.

Metric: Gromov-Wasserstein distance d_{GW}(\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j)

Properties: Invariant to orthogonal transformations and isometric embeddings; sensitive to scale confounds; may not survive calibration.

Level III: Structural Convergence (Strongest Claim)

Models independently recover the same underlying causal, mathematical, or physical relationships:

\operatorname{Structure}(R_i) \cong \operatorname{Structure}(R_j) \cong S

Metric: Persistent Homology Bottleneck Distance; Interventions Commutativity Error; Relational Profile Equivalence (Yoneda embedding)

Properties: Invariant to non-linear diffeomorphisms; preserves topological invariants; requires causal testing.

9.1 Formalizing Level III Convergence

CRH v0.3 formalizes Level III convergence as a commutativity between representation dynamics and environmental dynamics.

Let the environment \mathcal{E} be governed by a latent state transition operator:

\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{S}

Level III convergence exists if there exists a feature mapping \phi_i: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{M}_i and a latent transition operator \mathcal{T}_{R_i} such that the following diagram commutes within an \epsilon-bound:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{S} & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}} & \mathcal{S} \\
\downarrow{\phi_i} & & \downarrow{\phi_i} \\
\mathcal{M}_i & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{R_i}} & \mathcal{M}_i
\end{array}

\left\Vert \phi_i(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, a)) - \mathcal{T}_{R_i}(\phi_i(S), a) \right\Vert_{\mathcal{M}_i} \le \epsilon(C)

Level III convergence between two models i and j requires that their respective transition operators \mathcal{T}_{R_i} and \mathcal{T}_{R_j} are equivalent under an admissible transformation, and that both commute with the environmental dynamics.

9.2 The Relational Profile Alternative

The Indra framework offers an alternative formalization of convergence that does not require geometric alignment at all. Under this view, the representation of entity x in model i is defined not by a vector R_i(x), but by a functor:

\Phi_i(x): \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{V}

assigning to each entity y in the environmental category \mathcal{C} a measure of relatedness to x, valued in a monoidal category \mathcal{V}.

Convergence between models i and j is then measured by the naturality of the transformation between \Phi_i and \Phi_j, rather than by geometric distance.

CRH incorporates this perspective as a complementary formalization. The geometric and relational formulations are not competitors but dual perspectives on the same phenomenon: geometric convergence corresponds to approximate preservation of pairwise distances, while relational convergence corresponds to approximate preservation of relational profiles. Both should be tested, and convergence claims should be robust to both formalizations.

