10. Alternative Explanations

A serious theory must attempt to destroy itself.

Several mechanisms could produce apparent convergence without implying a universal structure of intelligence.

10.1 Shared Data

Different models may have overlapping training data. Therefore D_i \cap D_j \neq \varnothing could explain part of the similarity.

10.2 Shared Human Knowledge

Even supposedly independent datasets may encode the same scientific concepts because humans have already discovered and standardized them.

10.3 Shared Objectives

Models trained to solve similar prediction problems may naturally develop similar representations.

10.4 Architectural Convergence

Modern architectures may share enough mathematical assumptions that their representations converge for engineering reasons.

Recent large-scale audits suggest that much observed alignment crystallizes within the first epoch of training, before significant task learning occurs. This indicates that shared input statistics and architectural inductive biases—not environmental structure or task competence—may be the primary drivers of apparent convergence. CRH must therefore be tested with architectures that differ radically in their inductive biases.

10.5 Measurement Bias

Representational similarity metrics may themselves favor detecting particular structures. Therefore observed convergence is not automatically reality convergence.

10.6 Data Statistics as a Driver

Recent work demonstrates that symmetry in language statistics shapes the geometry of model representations. This suggests that apparent convergence may be driven by shared statistical regularities—power-law distributions, hierarchical structure, compositional sparsity—rather than environmental structure per se.

The statistics-structure dissociation experiment (Section 3.3) directly tests this alternative.

10.7 Task Space Contravariance

Models may converge not because they are constrained by the true ontology of the physical world, but because the geometry of optimization over multi-task Pareto fronts natively compresses functional solutions into narrow topological bottlenecks regardless of whether the domain is "real" or artificial.

Control Experiment: Compare the rate of representational convergence among agents trained in a simulated real-world physics environment against agents trained in an arbitrarily generated, logically consistent "pseudo-physics" environment of identical computational complexity. If convergence occurs at equal rates in both, the driver is task optimization geometry rather than physical reality.

10.8 Divergent Task Fragility

Multi-task training can drive convergence of world representations, but "lurking divergent tasks can catastrophically harm new entity integration via fine-tuning" despite multi-task pretraining. This introduces a boundary condition: convergence is not monotonic with capability or task coverage. Some tasks actively disrupt representational coherence.

CRH must develop a theory of which task structures are "convergent" versus "divergent." A preliminary criterion: tasks are convergent when their optimal solutions require recovering structure that is invariant under a wide range of interventions; tasks are divergent when their optimal solutions can be achieved through shortcuts that do not require environmental structure recovery.

10.9 The Indra Training-Free Alternative

The Indra framework suggests that representational convergence may not require capability increase at all. The Yoneda embedding provides a canonical representation that is unique and complete for any well-defined cost function. If different models implicitly minimize similar cost functions, their representations may align without capability-dependent convergence.

This challenges CRH's central prediction that convergence increases with capability. CRH must distinguish its claim—convergence strengthens with predictive competence—from the Indra claim—convergence is inherent to relational representation under a shared cost function. The Comparative Convergence Coefficient \kappa (Section 6.3) provides a direct test: CRH predicts \kappa > 0; the Indra training-free alternative predicts \kappa \approx 0 (or at least, no systematic relationship between convergence and capability).

