12. The Convergence Attractor

A stronger theoretical formulation introduces the concept of a representational attractor.

Let R_i(t) represent the internal organization of model i as capability increases.

CRH proposes the possibility of an attractor region \mathcal{A}_S associated with the underlying structure S. Then:

R_i(t) \rightarrow \mathcal{A}_S

for sufficiently capable systems.

Importantly, this attractor need not be a single point. It may be an equivalence class:

\mathcal{A}_S = \{T(S) : T \in \mathcal{G}\}

where \mathcal{G} is a group of permissible representational transformations.

12.1 Refinement: Coarse Attractor, Fine Freedom

CRH v0.3 refines the attractor concept to reflect the granularity hierarchy:

The attractor region \mathcal{A}_S is narrower for coarse local neighborhood structure than for fine-grained local structure, which is in turn narrower than for global geometric structure. Models are more strongly constrained in which coarse categories are similar to which than in how individual instances relate, and more strongly constrained in instance-level relationships than in global distance structure.

This formulation avoids the naïve claim that all intelligent systems must literally possess identical internal states. Instead, different intelligences may occupy different coordinates within the same structural equivalence class—and the equivalence class is defined primarily by coarse relational topology.

12.2 Attractor Dynamics and the Indra Perspective

The Indra framework suggests that the attractor is not a region in a geometric space but a canonical relational profile defined by the Yoneda embedding. Under this view, convergence is not movement toward a geometric configuration, but improvement in the accuracy with which each model's internal representations capture the environmental relational structure.

This perspective resolves a potential tension: if the attractor is a geometric region, convergence requires specifying a metric on representation space. If the attractor is a relational profile, convergence requires only that entities stand in well-defined relationships—which is the minimal assumption needed for the convergence hypothesis to be meaningful.

