13. Toward a General Theory of Intelligence

If CRH survives empirical testing, it would suggest a profound reinterpretation of intelligence.

Intelligence might not primarily be the ability to construct arbitrary internal worlds. It might instead be the ability to progressively approximate invariant structure under environmental constraints.

We could therefore define intelligence abstractly as:

I = F(\text{prediction}, \text{compression}, \text{causal inference}, \text{adaptation})

subject to:

R \approx S

Under this interpretation, intelligence is constrained by the structure it attempts to model. Different implementations may therefore converge toward common mathematical organizations.

13.1 The Functional Correspondence Requirement

Representational similarity measures should be evaluated through the lens of functional correspondence—whether models make similar decisions—rather than purely geometric alignment.

CRH v0.3 incorporates this requirement: structural convergence claims must be validated by demonstrating that geometrically aligned representations also exhibit functional and causal correspondence. This prevents the framework from claiming convergence on the basis of geometric similarity that has no functional significance.

13.2 The Relational Profile Requirement

The Indra framework suggests an additional requirement: convergence claims should be validated at the level of relational profiles, not merely pairwise distances. Two models may have similar pairwise distance matrices but different relational structures—for example, one model might represent a linear chain while another represents a hierarchical tree with similar pairwise distances.

CRH v0.3 therefore requires that convergence claims be robust to both geometric and relational formalizations. A convergence claim is strengthened when it holds under both the Gromov-Wasserstein distance and the Yoneda embedding comparison.

