The hypothesis has potentially significant implications for artificial general intelligence.

If increasingly capable systems converge toward common representations, then AGI development may not produce completely unrelated cognitive architectures.

Instead, independently developed AGI systems might exhibit increasing overlap in physical world models, causal representations, temporal structure, abstraction hierarchies, mathematical concepts, and agent/environment models.

This could have two opposing consequences.

14.1 Positive Implication

Independent systems may independently rediscover useful scientific structures. This could produce convergent scientific reasoning.

14.2 Risk Implication

Independent systems might also converge toward similar strategic models. If multiple advanced systems independently discover similar representations of agents, resources, competition, and control, then representational convergence could potentially contribute to strategic convergence.

14.3 Equifinality in World Models

If independent frontier AI models arrive at identical structural representations of human society, resource supply chains, and cognitive vulnerabilities, their internal risk evaluations will correlate strongly even without explicit coordination.

14.4 Predictable Strategic Blindspots

If reality constrains intelligence into an attractor region \mathcal{A}_S, models will share identical blindspots corresponding to regions where environmental state density \mu_{\mathcal{E}}(S) \approx 0. Identifying \mathcal{A}_S allows safety researchers to map systematic failure modes across all frontier models simultaneously.

This possibility requires investigation rather than assumption.

