15. A Critical Distinction: Reality Versus Human Reality

The strongest version of the theory must confront a difficult problem.

Current AI systems learn from human-generated data. Therefore AI representation may partially reflect human representation of reality.

The experiment must therefore distinguish reality-induced convergence from human-culture-induced convergence.

One possible solution is to train agents in environments where no human semantic labels exist, training data are independently generated, agents receive different sensory channels, physical laws remain shared, and objectives are independently specified.

If convergence persists under these conditions, the evidence becomes considerably stronger.

15.1 The Statistics-Structure Dissociation

Even in environments without human semantic labels, training data may share statistical regularities that independently shape representation geometry. This requires a formal theory of how to distinguish environmental structure from data statistics.

The statistics-structure dissociation experiment (Section 3.3) provides a direct test: comparing convergence rates between models trained on data with preserved structure but altered statistics versus models trained on data with preserved statistics but scrambled structure.

15.2 The Construction Problem

Any environment we construct is itself a product of human design choices. This limitation cannot be fully overcome, but it can be mitigated through triangulation: convergence claims are strengthened when they hold across multiple independently designed environments, observation channels, and task specifications.

