16. The Ultimate Experiment

A decisive experiment would involve many independently constructed artificial agents.

Each agent would receive training data with minimal overlap. Each would use a substantially different architecture with non-overlapping inductive biases. Each would employ independently selected optimization procedures. Each would interact with the same underlying physical simulator.

The agents would be asked to predict increasingly difficult properties of the environment.

At regular capability intervals, researchers would extract representations R_i(C) and measure D_{ij}(C) while controlling for architecture, dataset overlap, random initialization, optimization, task design, measurement method, and metric confounds.

The critical result would be D_{ij}(C) \downarrow as C \uparrow, after metric calibration.

A particularly powerful result would be convergence toward structures independently recoverable from the known laws governing the environment. That would provide evidence that the models are not merely converging on one another—they are converging on the environment.

16.1 Enhanced Experimental Design

CRH v0.3 incorporates critical controls identified in recent literature:

1. Metric Calibration: All similarity measurements must use null-calibrated scores.
2. Architecture Diversity: Include at least 3–4 fundamentally different architectural families with non-overlapping inductive biases.
3. Early-Training Controls: Measure alignment at initialization, after one epoch, and at convergence to distinguish architectural-prior convergence from capability-driven convergence.
4. Divergent Task Controls: Explicitly test whether adding certain task types disrupts convergence.
5. Functional Correspondence Checks: Verify that geometrically aligned representations also exhibit functional and causal correspondence.
6. Scale-Robustness Testing: Evaluate cross-modal and cross-model alignment at multiple dataset scales (1K, 10K, 100K, 1M+ samples). If convergence is genuine, it should persist—or strengthen—with increased sample diversity. If convergence is an artifact of small-sample evaluation, it will degrade at scale.
7. Statistics-Structure Dissociation: Compare convergence rates between models trained on natural data, models trained on data with preserved statistics but scrambled structure, and models trained on data with preserved structure but altered statistics.
8. Relational Profile Verification: Test convergence using both geometric (Gromov-Wasserstein) and relational (Yoneda embedding) formalizations.
9. Many-to-Many Evaluation: Prior evaluations of cross-modal convergence have relied on one-to-one image-caption pairings. CRH must be tested under conditions that reflect realistic many-to-many mappings.

