17. Possible Failure Modes

CRH should be considered falsified or substantially weakened if experiments demonstrate that:

1. Representational similarity does not increase with capability after metric calibration (i.e., \kappa \leq 0);
2. Convergence disappears when datasets are made independent and input statistics are controlled;
3. Convergence occurs only among architecturally similar models with overlapping inductive biases;
4. Convergence metrics fail to survive transformations or null-calibration;
5. Apparent convergence does not correspond to environmental invariants or functional correspondence;
6. Highly capable systems consistently develop fundamentally incompatible structural representations;
7. Convergence is entirely explained by early-training architectural priors, with no additional convergence attributable to task competence;
8. Divergent tasks disrupt convergence in ways that cannot be predicted by environmental structure;
9. Coarse-grained cross-modal convergence degrades at scale, suggesting that even the robust claim is an artifact of small-sample evaluation;
10. Convergence is equally strong in pseudo-physics and real-physics environments, suggesting that task optimization geometry, not environmental structure, is the operative mechanism;
11. Convergence is stronger in preserved-statistics conditions than in preserved-structure conditions, suggesting that data statistics, not environmental structure, drive the phenomenon.

A strong theory must permit the possibility of being wrong. Otherwise it is merely philosophy wearing a laboratory coat.

