19. Toward a Mathematical Theory of Representational Universality

The ultimate goal should not be to catalogue similarities between models. The deeper objective is to determine whether there exists a mathematical object \mathcal{U} representing the invariant structure that sufficiently capable learning systems tend to approximate.

We could then investigate R_i \overset{T_i}{\sim} \mathcal{U}, where T_i represents model-specific transformations.

The central theoretical question becomes:

\boxed{\exists\ \mathcal{U} \quad \text{such that} \quad \lim_{C\rightarrow\infty} D(T_i(R_i), \mathcal{U}) = 0?}

19.1 Refined Formulation: Relational Category

CRH v0.3 refines \mathcal{U} as a relational category rather than a geometric manifold:

\mathcal{U} \cong \text{Yoneda}(\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{E}})

where \mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{E}} is the category of environmental entities under the environmental cost function.

The convergence metric should be evaluated using both the Gromov-Wasserstein distance and the relational profile equivalence:

\lim_{C \to \infty} d_{GW}\left( (\mathcal{M}_i, d_i, \mu_i), (\mathcal{U}, d_{\mathcal{U}}, \mu_{\mathcal{U}}) \right) = 0

\lim_{C \to \infty} \text{RelEquiv}(\Phi_i, \Phi_{\mathcal{U}}) = 0

19.2 The Granularity Caveat

CRH v0.3 explicitly notes that \mathcal{U} is best understood not as a single global coordinate system, but as an equivalence class of relational structures organized by granularity. The universal relational category encodes which coarse categories are similar to which more robustly than which fine-grained instances are similar to which, and more robustly than how far apart entities are in absolute distance terms.

If such a structure exists, it would constitute something resembling a Universal Representation Manifold—though "Universal Relational Category" may be the more precise term.

This would not mean that every intelligence literally thinks identically. It would mean that sufficiently capable intelligences may independently discover the same underlying relational structure—at least at the level of coarse local topology.

