20. Conclusion

The Platonic Representation Hypothesis suggests a remarkable possibility: artificial intelligence systems may not remain indefinitely trapped inside arbitrary representational worlds.

As capability increases, they may increasingly discover common structures imposed by the environment they are attempting to model.

The strongest version of this proposition remains unproven. Nevertheless, it generates a concrete research program.

20.1 The Precision Move

CRH v0.3 recasts the hypothesis as a theory of coarse local-structural convergence first, with fine-grained and global convergence as stronger, contingent claims. This is not a retreat—it is a precision move that makes the hypothesis more defensible and more interesting.

The key distinctions are between identical representations and structurally equivalent representations, and between coarse local structure, fine-grained local structure, and global geometric structure.

The former distinctions are neither necessary nor particularly interesting. The latter could be profound.

20.2 The Central Question

If independently constructed intelligences repeatedly converge toward the same coarse relational structures despite different architectures, datasets, objectives, and sensory modalities, then representational convergence may reflect something deeper than engineering convention.

It may indicate that reality constrains intelligence.

And if increasingly capable minds are forced toward common structural descriptions of the world, then intelligence may possess something analogous to an attractor landscape.

Different minds could begin from radically different locations. But the structure of reality may gradually narrow the paths available to them.

20.3 The Answer So Far

The ultimate question is therefore not "Will all AIs think the same way?"

It is: "How much freedom does intelligence actually have to represent a structured reality differently?"

The answer, based on the most recent evidence, appears to be:

More freedom than the Platonic view suggests, but less than arbitrary-relativism would claim. Cross-modal convergence is far more constrained than intra-modal convergence. Coarse-grained convergence appears more robust than fine-grained convergence. And the role of capability in driving convergence—as distinct from architectural priors, data statistics, and task geometry—remains an open question that the Comparative Convergence Coefficient is designed to answer.

That question is experimentally accessible. It is mathematically formalizable. And, most importantly, it is falsifiable.

That makes it worth investigating.

20.4 The Theoretical Contribution Without Experimentation

This document makes no empirical claims. It reports no experiments. It presents no data.

Its contribution is theoretical: a framework for distinguishing genuine reality-constrained convergence from the multiple confounds that can produce apparent convergence. The framework provides definitions, distinctions, predictions, and a quantitative coefficient that makes the hypothesis falsifiable.

The value of a theoretical framework lies not in its immediate empirical support but in its capacity to guide future research, to sharpen questions, to expose hidden assumptions, and to provide criteria for what would count as evidence for or against the hypothesis.

CRH v0.3 aims to provide exactly this: a precise, calibrated, and falsifiable statement of the claim that intelligence is constrained by the structure of reality—and a roadmap for testing whether that claim is true.

The experiments are for others to conduct. The theory is offered here.


