Appendix A: Formal Definitions and Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Basic Objects

A.1.1 Environmental State Space

Let (\mathcal{S}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{S}}, \mu_{\mathcal{S}}) be a probability space representing the latent environmental state space, where:

· \mathcal{S} is the set of possible environmental states
· \Sigma_{\mathcal{S}} is a \sigma-algebra on \mathcal{S}
· \mu_{\mathcal{S}} is a probability measure representing the natural distribution of environmental states

The measure \mu_{\mathcal{S}} encodes the frequency with which different environmental configurations occur. This is distinct from the structure of \mathcal{S} itself: two environments may share identical structural laws but differ in \mu_{\mathcal{S}} if some states are more common than others.

Definition A.1 (Environmental Structure). The environmental structure is the set of invariant relationships that hold across all states S \in \mathcal{S}, independent of the measure \mu_{\mathcal{S}}. Formally, structure is encoded in the transition operator \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}} (defined below) and the observation function g, not in the sampling distribution.

This distinction is crucial for the statistics-structure dissociation experiment: altering \mu_{\mathcal{S}} changes statistics but preserves structure; altering the laws governing \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}} preserves statistics but changes structure.

A.1.2 Observation Space

Let (\mathcal{X}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{X}}) be a measurable space representing observations. Observations are generated by a measurable function:

g: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{X}

The observation process may be stochastic, in which case we consider a Markov kernel:

\kappa_g: \mathcal{S} \times \Sigma_{\mathcal{X}} \to [0,1]

Definition A.2 (Observation-Induced Equivalence). Two states S_1, S_2 \in \mathcal{S} are observationally equivalent if g(S_1) = g(S_2) (or, in the stochastic case, if \kappa_g(S_1, \cdot) = \kappa_g(S_2, \cdot)).

The quotient space \mathcal{S} / \sim_g, where \sim_g is observational equivalence, represents the set of distinctions that are in principle recoverable from observations. States that are observationally equivalent cannot be distinguished by any learning system, regardless of capability.

A.1.3 Learning System

A learning system M is a tuple:

M = (A, D, O, \theta, f)

where:

· A: architecture (a class of parameterized functions)
· D: training data distribution (a probability measure on \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y})
· O: optimization objective (a functional to be minimized)
· \theta \in \Theta: learned parameters
· f_\theta: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Z}: representation function, where \mathcal{Z} is the representation space

Definition A.3 (Representation Manifold). For a learning system M_i, the representation manifold \mathcal{M}_i is defined as:

\mathcal{M}_i = \{f_{\theta_i}(x) : x \in \text{supp}(\mu_{\mathcal{X}})\}

equipped with a metric d_i: \mathcal{M}_i \times \mathcal{M}_i \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}.

In practice, \mathcal{M}_i is approximated by the set of representations of a finite sample of observations. The quality of this approximation depends on sample size and the smoothness of f_{\theta_i}.

A.2 Metric Spaces and Measure Theory

A.2.1 Metric Measure Spaces

A metric measure space (mm-space) is a triple (X, d, \mu) where:

· (X, d) is a metric space
· \mu is a Borel probability measure on X

All representation manifolds \mathcal{M}_i are treated as mm-spaces. The measure \mu_i is the push-forward of the environmental measure:

\mu_i = (f_{\theta_i} \circ g)_\# \mu_{\mathcal{S}}

This ensures that the measure on the representation space reflects the distribution of environmental states, not the distribution of training samples.

A.2.2 Gromov-Wasserstein Distance

For two mm-spaces (X, d_X, \mu_X) and (Y, d_Y, \mu_Y), the Gromov-Wasserstein distance of order p is defined as:

d_{GW,p}(X, Y) = \inf_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu_X, \mu_Y)} \left( \int_{X \times Y} \int_{X \times Y} |d_X(x, x') - d_Y(y, y')|^p \, d\pi(x,y) \, d\pi(x',y') \right)^{1/p}

where \Pi(\mu_X, \mu_Y) is the set of all couplings of \mu_X and \mu_Y.

Key properties:

1. d_{GW,p}(X, Y) = 0 if and only if X and Y are isometric as mm-spaces
2. Invariant under isometries of the individual spaces
3. Does not require alignment of underlying coordinates
4. Satisfies the triangle inequality

Theorem A.1 (GW Triangle Inequality). For any three mm-spaces X, Y, Z:

d_{GW,p}(X, Z) \leq d_{GW,p}(X, Y) + d_{GW,p}(Y, Z)

Proof. Standard result in metric geometry. The proof follows from the gluing lemma for optimal couplings and the triangle inequality for the underlying metrics. \square

A.2.3 Wasserstein Distance

For two probability measures \mu and \nu on a metric space (X, d), the Wasserstein distance of order p is:

W_p(\mu, \nu) = \left( \inf_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu, \nu)} \int_{X \times X} d(x, y)^p \, d\pi(x, y) \right)^{1/p}

Theorem A.2 (Kantorovich-Rubinstein Duality). For p = 1:

W_1(\mu, \nu) = \sup_{\|f\|_{\text{Lip}} \leq 1} \left| \int_X f \, d\mu - \int_X f \, d\nu \right|

where \|f\|_{\text{Lip}} denotes the Lipschitz constant of f.

Proof. Standard result in optimal transport theory. \square

A.3 Topological Tools

A.3.1 Persistent Homology

For a filtration of simplicial complexes:

\emptyset = K_0 \subseteq K_1 \subseteq \cdots \subseteq K_n = K

the k-th persistence diagram \text{dgm}_k is a multiset of points (b, d) \in \mathbb{R}^2 where b is the birth time and d is the death time of a k-dimensional homology class.

Definition A.4 (Vietoris-Rips Filtration). For a finite metric space (X, d), the Vietoris-Rips complex at scale \epsilon is:

\text{VR}_\epsilon(X) = \{\sigma \subseteq X : \text{diam}(\sigma) \leq 2\epsilon\}

The Vietoris-Rips filtration is the family \{\text{VR}_\epsilon(X)\}_{\epsilon \geq 0}.

A.3.2 Bottleneck Distance

The bottleneck distance between two persistence diagrams D_1 and D_2 is:

d_B(D_1, D_2) = \inf_{\gamma: D_1 \to D_2} \sup_{p \in D_1} \|p - \gamma(p)\|_\infty

where \gamma ranges over all bijections between D_1 and D_2 (with diagonal points added as needed).

Theorem A.3 (Stability of Persistent Homology). Let X and Y be totally bounded metric spaces. Then for any k:

d_B(\text{dgm}_k(X), \text{dgm}_k(Y)) \leq d_{GH}(X, Y)

where d_{GH} is the Gromov-Hausdorff distance.

Proof. This is the fundamental stability theorem for persistent homology, first proved by Cohen-Steiner, Edelsbrunner, and Harer. The proof constructs an explicit matching between persistence diagrams using the interleaving distance between persistence modules. \square

Corollary A.3.1. If d_{GW}(\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j) \to 0, then d_B(\text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_i), \text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_j)) \to 0 for all k.

Proof. The Gromov-Wasserstein distance dominates the Gromov-Hausdorff distance (for normalized measures), which in turn dominates the bottleneck distance by Theorem A.3. \square

A.3.3 Betti Numbers

The k-th Betti number \beta_k of a topological space X is the rank of its k-th homology group H_k(X):

\beta_k(X) = \text{rank}(H_k(X))

Interpretation:

· \beta_0: number of connected components
· \beta_1: number of one-dimensional holes (cycles)
· \beta_2: number of two-dimensional voids

A.4 Causal Structure

A.4.1 Structural Causal Models

A structural causal model (SCM) over variables V = \{V_1, \ldots, V_n\} is a tuple (\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F}, P_U) where:

· \mathcal{U}: exogenous noise variables
· \mathcal{F} = \{f_1, \ldots, f_n\}: structural equations V_i = f_i(\text{Pa}(V_i), U_i)
· P_U: distribution over \mathcal{U}

A.4.2 Interventions

An intervention \text{do}(X = x) modifies the SCM by replacing the structural equation for X with X = x.

The interventional distribution is denoted:

P(Y | \text{do}(X = x))

Definition A.5 (Identifiability). A causal effect P(Y | \text{do}(X = x)) is identifiable from observational data if it can be uniquely computed from the joint distribution P(V) and the causal graph.

Theorem A.4 (Do-Calculus). The rules of do-calculus provide necessary and sufficient conditions for identifiability in a given causal graph.

Proof. See Pearl (2009), Chapter 3. \square

A.4.3 Causal Commutativity

For a representation R_i = f_i \circ g, we say the representation preserves causal structure if the following diagram commutes:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{S} & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}} & \mathcal{S} \\
\downarrow{\phi_i} & & \downarrow{\phi_i} \\
\mathcal{M}_i & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{R_i}} & \mathcal{M}_i
\end{array}

where \phi_i = f_i \circ g and \mathcal{T}_{R_i} is the induced transition operator on the representation space.

