Appendix B: Metric Calibration Framework

B.1 The Problem of Metric Confounding

B.1.1 Statement of the Problem

Recent work has demonstrated that global representational similarity metrics are systematically confounded by network scale. Specifically:

1. Increasing model depth or width can inflate similarity scores even when representation spaces are unrelated
2. Eigenvalue concentration in high-dimensional representations creates apparent spectral alignment
3. Apparent global convergence largely disappears after null-calibration

B.1.2 Formalization of the Confound

Let R \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d} be a representation matrix where n is the number of samples and d is the dimensionality.

The empirical covariance matrix is:

\hat{\Sigma} = \frac{1}{n-1} R^T R

For high-dimensional representations (d \to \infty with n fixed), the eigenvalues of \hat{\Sigma} concentrate under certain conditions. This concentration can create spurious similarity between unrelated representations.

B.1.3 Theorem: Scale-Induced Similarity Inflation

Theorem B.1. Let R_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_1} and R_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_2} be two independent random matrices with i.i.d. standard normal entries. Let \text{CKA}(R_1, R_2) be the Centered Kernel Alignment between them. Then:

\mathbb{E}[\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2)] > 0

for finite n and d_1, d_2, with the expectation increasing as n decreases relative to d_1, d_2.

Proof. The CKA between two representations is defined as:

\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2) = \frac{\text{HSIC}(K_1, K_2)}{\sqrt{\text{HSIC}(K_1, K_1) \cdot \text{HSIC}(K_2, K_2)}}

where K_1 = R_1 R_1^T and K_2 = R_2 R_2^T are the Gram matrices.

For independent random matrices, the expected HSIC is not zero for finite samples due to the centering operation. The centering matrix H = I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^T introduces correlations between otherwise independent entries.

Specifically:

\mathbb{E}[\text{HSIC}(K_1, K_2)] = \frac{\text{Tr}(H \mathbb{E}[K_1] H \mathbb{E}[K_2])}{(n-1)^2} = O(1/n)

for independent representations, while the denominator scales as:

\sqrt{\mathbb{E}[\text{HSIC}(K_1, K_1)] \cdot \mathbb{E}[\text{HSIC}(K_2, K_2)]} = O\left(\frac{\sqrt{d_1 d_2}}{n}\right)

Thus:

\mathbb{E}[\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2)] = O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_1 d_2}}\right)

which is positive for finite dimensions. \square

Corollary B.1.1. The expected CKA between independent random representations increases as the sample size n decreases relative to the dimensionality d. This creates apparent similarity between unrelated representations when evaluated on small samples.

Corollary B.1.2. For fixed sample size n, increasing dimensionality d decreases the spurious CKA—but only because the denominator grows faster than the numerator. This means that comparing models of different dimensionalities can create systematic biases.

B.2 Null-Calibration Procedure

B.2.1 Permutation-Based Null Distribution

For representations R_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_1} and R_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_2}, the null distribution is constructed by:

1. Generating B random permutations \{\pi_b\}_{b=1}^B of \{1, \ldots, n\}
2. Computing the similarity metric s_b = \text{Sim}(R_1, R_2[\pi_b]) for each permutation
3. The null distribution is \hat{F}_0(s) = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \mathbf{1}[s_b \leq s]

Theorem B.2 (Exchangeability Under Null). Under the null hypothesis that representations are independent, the permuted samples are exchangeable, and the permutation test provides exact control of the Type I error rate.

Proof. This follows from the exchangeability of independent samples. If R_1 and R_2 are independent, then R_2[\pi] is exchangeable with R_2 for any permutation \pi. The permutation test therefore provides exact control of the Type I error rate. \square

B.2.2 Calibrated Similarity Score

The calibrated similarity score is defined as:

s_{\text{cal}} = \frac{s_{\text{obs}} - \hat{\mu}_0}{\hat{\sigma}_0}

where:

· s_{\text{obs}} is the observed similarity
· \hat{\mu}_0 is the mean of the null distribution
· \hat{\sigma}_0 is the standard deviation of the null distribution

Interpretation: s_{\text{cal}} represents the number of standard deviations by which the observed similarity exceeds the expected similarity under the null hypothesis of independence. A value of 0 indicates no evidence of convergence beyond chance; positive values indicate convergence; negative values indicate divergence.

B.2.3 False Discovery Rate Control

For multiple comparisons across model pairs, the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure is applied to control the false discovery rate at level \alpha = 0.05.

Procedure:

1. Sort p-values: p_{(1)} \leq p_{(2)} \leq \cdots \leq p_{(m)}
2. Find the largest k such that p_{(k)} \leq \frac{k}{m} \alpha
3. Reject all hypotheses with p \leq p_{(k)}

Theorem B.3 (Benjamini-Hochberg Control). Under independence of test statistics, the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure controls the false discovery rate at level \alpha.

Proof. See Benjamini & Hochberg (1995). \square

B.3 Metric-Specific Calibration

B.3.1 Calibrated CKA

Definition. For representations R_1 and R_2, the calibrated CKA is:

\text{CKA}_{\text{cal}}(R_1, R_2) = \frac{\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2) - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2[\pi])]}{1 - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\text{CKA}(R_1, R_2[\pi])]}

where \mathbb{E}_\pi is the expectation over random permutations.

Theorem B.4. If R_1 and R_2 are independent, then \text{CKA}_{\text{cal}}(R_1, R_2) \to 0 as B \to \infty.

Proof. Follows directly from the definition: the expected CKA under permutation equals the observed CKA when representations are exchangeable, so the numerator tends to zero. \square

B.3.2 Local Neighborhood Similarity

Definition. For a representation R, the k-nearest neighbor graph G_k(R) is constructed by connecting each point to its k nearest neighbors in representation space.

The neighborhood similarity between R_1 and R_2 is:

\text{NS}_k(R_1, R_2) = \frac{|E(G_k(R_1)) \cap E(G_k(R_2))|}{|E(G_k(R_1)) \cup E(G_k(R_2))|}

where E(G) is the edge set of graph G.

B.3.3 Theoretical Properties of Neighborhood Similarity

Theorem B.5. The neighborhood similarity \text{NS}_k is invariant under monotone transformations of distances within each representation space.

Proof. The k-nearest neighbor graph depends only on the ordering of distances, not their absolute values. Any monotone transformation preserves this ordering. \square

Corollary B.5.1. \text{NS}_k is invariant under scaling, translation, and any homeomorphism applied independently to each representation space.

This property makes \text{NS}_k more robust to coordinate choices than global metrics.

Theorem B.6 (Expected Null Neighborhood Similarity). For two independent random representations of n points, the expected neighborhood similarity is:

\mathbb{E}[\text{NS}_k] = \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}} \cdot \frac{2k}{2n - k - 1}

for the Jaccard index formulation, where \binom{n}{k} represents the number of possible k-neighbor sets.

Proof (Sketch). Under independence, the k-nearest neighbor graphs are random subsets of edges. The expected overlap can be computed combinatorially. The exact formula depends on the specific graph structure (directed vs. undirected, exact k vs. at most k). \square

Corollary B.6.1. The null neighborhood similarity decreases with n and increases with k. This must be accounted for in calibration.

B.4 The Granularity Hierarchy

B.4.1 Formal Statement

Definition (Coarse-Grained Neighborhood). For a representation R and a partition \mathcal{P} = \{P_1, \ldots, P_m\} of the sample space, the coarse-grained k-nearest neighbor graph G_k^{\mathcal{P}}(R) connects points in the same partition element if they are mutual k-nearest neighbors, and connects partition elements if any member of one is a k-nearest neighbor of any member of the other.

Definition (Granularity Levels).

· Coarse granularity: \mathcal{P} corresponds to natural categories (e.g., object classes, semantic types)
· Fine granularity: \mathcal{P} corresponds to individual instances
· Global granularity: No partition; global distance structure

Theorem B.7 (Granularity Monotonicity). If two representations converge at fine granularity, they also converge at coarse granularity. The converse does not hold.

Proof. (Forward direction) Fine-grained convergence implies that instance-level neighborhoods agree, which implies that category-level neighborhoods agree (since category-level neighborhoods are determined by instance-level neighborhoods). (Converse failure) Two representations can have similar category-level structure but different instance-level organization within categories. \square

Theorem B.8 (Scale-Fragility of Fine-Grained Convergence). Under mild regularity conditions, fine-grained neighborhood similarity decreases as sample size increases, while coarse-grained neighborhood similarity remains stable or increases.

Proof (Informal). As sample size increases, the density of points in representation space increases. Fine-grained neighborhoods become increasingly sensitive to small perturbations in relative distances, while coarse-grained neighborhoods are determined by larger-scale structure that is more robust to such perturbations. \square

This theorem formalizes the empirical finding from the "Back into Plato's Cave" study that fine-grained alignment degrades at scale while coarse semantic alignment persists.

B.5 The Relational Profile Metric

B.5.1 Yoneda Embedding

Let \mathcal{C} be a category enriched over a monoidal category \mathcal{V}. The \mathcal{V}-enriched Yoneda embedding is the functor:

Y: \mathcal{C} \to [\mathcal{C}^{\text{op}}, \mathcal{V}]

defined by:

Y(c) = \mathcal{C}(-, c)

This assigns to each object c the functor that maps any object d to the hom-object \mathcal{C}(d, c).

Theorem B.9 (Yoneda Lemma). The Yoneda embedding is full and faithful. That is, for any objects c, d \in \mathcal{C}:

[\mathcal{C}^{\text{op}}, \mathcal{V}](Y(c), Y(d)) \cong \mathcal{C}(c, d)

Proof. This is the enriched Yoneda lemma. The proof constructs an explicit isomorphism using the identity morphism. \square

Corollary B.9.1. Two objects are isomorphic in \mathcal{C} if and only if their Yoneda embeddings are naturally isomorphic.

This means that the relational profile of an entity completely determines its structure: two entities are equivalent if and only if they relate to all other entities in equivalent ways.

B.5.2 Relational Profile Equivalence

Definition. For two learning systems i and j, the relational profile equivalence is:

\text{RelEquiv}(\Phi_i, \Phi_j) = \sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} d_{\mathcal{V}}(\Phi_i(x), \Phi_j(x))

where \Phi_i(x) is the relational profile of x in model i, and d_{\mathcal{V}} is an appropriate metric on the functor category.

Theorem B.10 (Relationship to GW Distance). If the relational profiles are computed using pairwise distances as the cost function, then relational profile equivalence is bounded above by the Gromov-Wasserstein distance.

Proof (Sketch). The Gromov-Wasserstein distance measures the discrepancy between pairwise distance matrices. The relational profile is a row (or column) of the distance matrix. Therefore, if the full distance matrices are similar (small GW distance), the individual relational profiles must also be similar. \square

