Appendix C: Formalizing Level III Convergence

C.1 Category-Theoretic Framework

C.1.1 Categories of Representations

Definition. The category \mathbf{Rep} has:

· Objects: Representation manifolds (\mathcal{M}, d, \mu)
· Morphisms: Measurable maps T: \mathcal{M}_1 \to \mathcal{M}_2 that are measure-preserving up to \epsilon

Definition. A morphism T is measure-preserving up to \epsilon if:

\|T_\# \mu_1 - \mu_2\|_{TV} \leq \epsilon

where \|\cdot\|_{TV} is the total variation distance.

C.1.2 Functorial Semantics

Definition. A representation functor F_i: \mathbf{Env} \to \mathbf{Rep} maps environmental structures to representation structures, where \mathbf{Env} is a category encoding environmental dynamics.

Objects of \mathbf{Env}: Environmental states \mathcal{S}

Morphisms of \mathbf{Env}: Environmental transitions \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}

C.1.3 Natural Transformations and Equivalence

Definition. Two representations F_i and F_j are naturally equivalent if there exists a natural transformation \eta: F_i \Rightarrow F_j such that each component \eta_S: F_i(S) \to F_j(S) is an isomorphism in \mathbf{Rep}.

Theorem C.1. Natural equivalence implies that the representations preserve the same environmental structure, up to isomorphism.

Proof. If \eta is a natural isomorphism, then for any environmental transition f: S \to S':

\eta_{S'} \circ F_i(f) = F_j(f) \circ \eta_S

This means that the representation of the transition f in model i corresponds (via \eta) to the representation of f in model j. \square

C.2 Dynamical Systems Commutativity

C.2.1 Environmental Dynamics

Let \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{S} be the environmental transition operator, where \mathcal{A} is the action space.

C.2.2 Representation Dynamics

For each model i, define a transition operator \mathcal{T}_{R_i}: \mathcal{M}_i \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{M}_i such that the following diagram approximately commutes:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{S} & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}} & \mathcal{S} \\
\downarrow{\phi_i} & & \downarrow{\phi_i} \\
\mathcal{M}_i & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{T}_{R_i}} & \mathcal{M}_i
\end{array}

C.2.3 Commutativity Error

Definition. The commutativity error for model i is:

\epsilon_i(C) = \mathbb{E}_{S \sim \mu_{\mathcal{S}}, a \sim \mu_{\mathcal{A}}} \left[ \left\| \phi_i(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, a)) - \mathcal{T}_{R_i}(\phi_i(S), a) \right\|_{\mathcal{M}_i} \right]

Theorem C.2 (Commutativity Error Bound). If the representation \phi_i is Lipschitz continuous with constant L_i, and the induced dynamics \mathcal{T}_{R_i} approximate the push-forward of \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}} under \phi_i, then:

\epsilon_i(C) \leq L_i \cdot \mathbb{E}[\|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}} - \hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathcal{E}}\|]

where \hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathcal{E}} is the pullback of \mathcal{T}_{R_i} to the state space.

Proof. By the Lipschitz property:

\|\phi_i(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, a)) - \mathcal{T}_{R_i}(\phi_i(S), a)\| \leq L_i \cdot \|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, a) - \phi_i^{-1}(\mathcal{T}_{R_i}(\phi_i(S), a))\|

Taking expectations yields the bound. \square

C.2.4 Level III Convergence Condition

Definition. Models i and j exhibit Level III convergence if there exists a transformation T_{ij}: \mathcal{M}_i \to \mathcal{M}_j such that:

1. T_{ij} \circ \mathcal{T}_{R_i} \approx \mathcal{T}_{R_j} \circ T_{ij} (dynamics preservation)
2. \epsilon_i(C) \to 0 and \epsilon_j(C) \to 0 as C \to \infty (environmental grounding)

Theorem C.3 (Transitivity of Level III Convergence). If model i exhibits Level III convergence with model j, and model j exhibits Level III convergence with model k, then model i exhibits Level III convergence with model k.

Proof. Let T_{ij} and T_{jk} be the transformations establishing convergence. Then T_{ik} = T_{jk} \circ T_{ij} establishes convergence between i and k. The commutativity errors compose via the triangle inequality. \square

C.3 Topological Invariants and Convergence

C.3.1 Persistent Homology of Representation Manifolds

For each representation manifold \mathcal{M}_i, compute the persistent homology \text{PH}_k(\mathcal{M}_i) for dimensions k = 0, 1, 2.

C.3.2 Convergence in Persistent Homology

Definition. Models i and j exhibit topological convergence if:

d_B(\text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_i), \text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_j)) < \epsilon

for all relevant k, where d_B is the bottleneck distance.

Theorem C.4 (GW Implies Topological Convergence). If d_{GW}(\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j) \to 0, then d_B(\text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_i), \text{dgm}_k(\mathcal{M}_j)) \to 0 for all k.

Proof. This follows from Corollary A.3.1 and the stability theorem for persistent homology. \square

Note: The converse does not hold. Topological convergence does not imply geometric convergence, since topology is invariant under a much larger class of transformations than isometry.

C.4 Causal Structure Preservation

C.4.1 Causal Commutativity Diagrams

For a system with intervention variables V, the representation preserves causal structure if:

P(R(Y) | \text{do}(R(X) = z)) = P(R(Y | \text{do}(X = x)))

whenever z = R(x).

C.4.2 Causal Convergence Metric

Definition. For models i and j, the causal convergence metric is:

\Delta_{\text{causal}}(i, j) = \sup_{X, Y, x} \left| P(R_i(Y) | \text{do}(R_i(X))) - T_{ij}\left[P(R_j(Y) | \text{do}(R_j(X)))\right] \right|

where T_{ij} is the representation transformation.

Theorem C.5 (Causal Convergence Bound). If models i and j exhibit Level III convergence, then their causal convergence metric is bounded by the commutativity errors:

\Delta_{\text{causal}}(i, j) \leq \epsilon_i(C) + \epsilon_j(C) + \|T_{ij} - T_{ij}^{-1}\|

Proof (Sketch). The causal convergence metric measures how well interventions in one representation correspond to interventions in the other. This is bounded by the commutativity errors plus the error in the transformation T_{ij}. \square

C.5 Relational Profile Convergence

C.5.1 Yoneda Embedding of Representation Manifolds

For each model i, define a functor:

\Phi_i: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbf{Met}

where \mathcal{C} is the environmental category and \mathbf{Met} is the category of metric spaces. This functor assigns to each environmental entity its relational profile in the representation space.

C.5.2 Relational Profile Convergence

Definition. Models i and j exhibit relational profile convergence if there exists a natural transformation \eta: \Phi_i \Rightarrow \Phi_j such that each component is an isomorphism up to \epsilon.

Theorem C.6 (Yoneda Completeness). If two models exhibit relational profile convergence with \epsilon = 0, then they are representationally equivalent: there is no structural information in one that is not in the other.

Proof. This follows from the Yoneda lemma: the relational profile completely determines the object up to isomorphism. \square

Theorem C.7 (Relationship to Geometric Convergence). Relational profile convergence is implied by Gromov-Wasserstein convergence, but not conversely.

Proof. (Forward) GW convergence implies that pairwise distances are approximately preserved, which implies that relational profiles are approximately preserved. (Converse failure) Relational profiles can be preserved even when some pairwise distances are not, since the profile captures local structure more robustly than global distances. \square

