Appendix E: Formalization of Alternative Explanations

E.1 Architectural Prior Confound

E.1.1 Formal Model

Let A_i be the architecture of model i. The representation can be decomposed as:

R_i = f_A(A_i) + f_D(D_i) + f_S(S) + \epsilon_i

where:

· f_A(A_i): architecture-specific component
· f_D(D_i): data-specific component
· f_S(S): environment-structure component
· \epsilon_i: noise

E.1.2 Testable Implication

If architectural priors dominate, then:

\text{Corr}(R_i, R_j) \gg \text{Corr}(f_S(S), R_i)

That is, the correlation between representations is greater than the correlation between representations and environmental structure.

E.1.3 Control Experiment

Train models with architectures A_1, \ldots, A_n on identical data. If convergence is driven by architecture, then:

d(R_i, R_j) \geq d(R_i, R_k)

whenever A_i and A_j are more similar than A_i and A_k.

E.2 Input Statistics Confound

E.2.1 Formal Model

If models are trained on data with similar statistics, their representations may converge due to shared input structure rather than environmental structure.

E.2.2 Control Experiment

Construct datasets D_1 and D_2 that differ in surface statistics but preserve underlying structure. If convergence persists, surface statistics are not the primary driver.

Conversely, construct datasets D_1' and D_2' that preserve statistics but scramble structure. If convergence persists here, statistics are the primary driver.

E.3 Task Space Contravariance

E.3.1 Formal Statement

Hypothesis. Optimization over multi-task Pareto fronts compresses functional solutions into narrow topological bottlenecks regardless of domain reality.

E.3.2 Control Experiment

Compare convergence rates between:

· Agents trained on real physics simulation
· Agents trained on "pseudo-physics" of identical computational complexity

If convergence rates are equal, the driver is task optimization geometry, not physical reality.

E.3.3 Formal Prediction

Under the contravariance hypothesis:

\frac{d\Gamma_{\text{real}}}{dC} = \frac{d\Gamma_{\text{pseudo}}}{dC}

Under CRH:

\frac{d\Gamma_{\text{real}}}{dC} > \frac{d\Gamma_{\text{pseudo}}}{dC}

Equivalently:

\kappa_{\text{real}} = \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} \quad \text{(Contravariance)}

\kappa_{\text{real}} > \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} \quad \text{(CRH)}

E.4 Early Crystallization

E.4.1 Formal Model

Decompose convergence into early and late components:

\Gamma(C) = \Gamma_{\text{early}}(C) + \Gamma_{\text{late}}(C)

where \Gamma_{\text{early}} is driven by architectural priors and \Gamma_{\text{late}} by environmental constraints.

E.4.2 Predictions

· If \Gamma_{\text{late}} \approx 0: convergence is entirely architectural (Mechanism 1)
· If \Gamma_{\text{late}} > 0: environmental constraint contributes (Mechanism 2)

E.4.3 Measurement Protocol

1. Measure \Gamma(0): alignment at initialization
2. Measure \Gamma(1): alignment after first epoch
3. Measure \Gamma(\infty): alignment at convergence

Then:

\Gamma_{\text{early}} = \Gamma(1) - \Gamma(0)

\Gamma_{\text{late}} = \Gamma(\infty) - \Gamma(1)

Theorem E.1 (Early Crystallization Test). If \Gamma_{\text{late}} = 0, then the Comparative Convergence Coefficient \kappa = 0, and CRH's Mechanism 2 is falsified.

Proof. If all convergence occurs early in training, then \Gamma(C) is constant for all C above the early-training threshold. Therefore \kappa = 0. \square

E.5 Data Statistics as a Driver

E.5.1 Formal Model

Recent work demonstrates that symmetry in language statistics shapes the geometry of model representations. Let \Sigma_D represent the statistical symmetries of the training data.

The representation can be decomposed as:

R_i = f_{\Sigma}(\Sigma_D) + f_S(S) + \epsilon_i

where f_{\Sigma} captures the influence of data statistics and f_S captures the influence of environmental structure.

E.5.2 Statistics-Structure Dissociation

Definition. The statistics-structure dissociation coefficient is:

\delta = \kappa_{\text{structure}} - \kappa_{\text{statistics}}

where:

· \kappa_{\text{structure}}: convergence rate for models trained on data with preserved structure but altered statistics
· \kappa_{\text{statistics}}: convergence rate for models trained on data with preserved statistics but scrambled structure

Theorem E.2 (Dissociation Test).

· If \delta > 0: environmental structure dominates. Supports CRH.
· If \delta < 0: data statistics dominate. Falsifies CRH.
· If \delta \approx 0: structure and statistics contribute equally. CRH requires qualification.

Proof. The coefficient \delta measures the differential contribution of structure versus statistics to convergence. Positive \delta indicates that convergence is stronger when structure is preserved, which is CRH's prediction. \square

E.6 The Indra Training-Free Alternative

E.6.1 Formal Statement

The Indra framework suggests that representational convergence may not require capability increase. The Yoneda embedding provides a canonical representation that is unique and complete for any well-defined cost function.

E.6.2 Testable Distinction

CRH predicts: \kappa > 0 (convergence increases with capability).

Indra predicts: \kappa \approx 0 (convergence is capability-independent).

Theorem E.3 (Capability Dependence Test). If \kappa > 0 for multiple independent model pairs, CRH is supported over the Indra training-free alternative. If \kappa \approx 0, the Indra alternative is supported.

E.7 Many-to-Many Realism

E.7.1 Formal Model

Prior evaluations of cross-modal convergence have relied on one-to-one image-caption pairings. Realistic perception involves many-to-many mappings: a single image supports multiple valid descriptions, and a single description corresponds to many possible images.

Definition. The many-to-many alignment score is:

\text{MM}(R_{\text{vision}}, R_{\text{text}}) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \text{TopK}(R_{\text{vision}}(x_i), R_{\text{text}}(y_{i,1}), \ldots, R_{\text{text}}(y_{i,m}))

where x_i is an image, y_{i,1}, \ldots, y_{i,m} are the m valid descriptions of x_i, and TopK measures whether the image representation is among the top-K nearest neighbors of at least one description representation.

Theorem E.4 (Many-to-Many Degradation). The many-to-many alignment score is lower than the one-to-one alignment score:

\text{MM}(R_{\text{vision}}, R_{\text{text}}) \leq \text{OneToOne}(R_{\text{vision}}, R_{\text{text}})

Proof. The one-to-one score is the special case of the many-to-many score where m = 1. Since the TopK condition is easier to satisfy with multiple candidates, the many-to-many score is more demanding. \square

