F.1 Phase 1: Synthetic Sandbox

F.1.1 Environment Specification

Procedural Physics Engine:

· 2D/3D rigid body dynamics
· Gravity, collisions, friction
· No human language or semantic labels
· Procedurally generated object shapes and properties

State Space \mathcal{S}:

· Object positions, velocities, masses, shapes
· Configurations sampled from a procedural generator
· Measure \mu_{\mathcal{S}} determined by the procedural generation algorithm

Observation Channels:

Agent Modality Dimensionality Notes
A RGB Pixels 256×256×3 High-resolution visual
B LiDAR Point Clouds Variable (1000–5000 points) Sparse depth
C Graph Distance Matrices N×N Object-relative distances

F.1.2 Architectures

Architecture Type Parameters Inductive Bias
Transformer Attention-based ~10M Permutation equivariance
Mamba State-space ~10M Sequential processing
Equivariant GNN Graph neural network ~10M Relational inductive bias

F.1.3 Training Protocol

· Independent datasets generated from same underlying simulator
· Minimal overlap between training examples
· Standardized optimization (AdamW, cosine schedule)
· Capability levels: C_1, \ldots, C_5 (increasing data, model capacity, or training duration)
· Capability measured by held-out prediction accuracy on physical invariants (e.g., object trajectories, collision outcomes)

F.1.4 Measurement Protocol

For each capability level and each pair of models:

1. Extract representations R_i and R_j
2. Compute calibrated similarity metrics
3. Compute Comparative Convergence Coefficient \kappa
4. Test for convergence using null-calibrated significance tests

F.2 Phase 2: Metric Deconfounding

F.2.1 Null Calibration

For each similarity metric s:

1. Compute observed similarity s_{\text{obs}}
2. Generate B = 1000 random permutations
3. Compute null distribution \{s_b\}_{b=1}^B
4. Calculate calibrated score s_{\text{cal}}

Implementation Details:

· Permutations should be independent across samples
· For cross-modal comparisons, permutations should be applied within each modality
· The null distribution should be estimated using the same sample size as the observed comparison

F.2.2 Local Topology Extraction

1. For each representation R, compute k-NN graph with k = 10, 20, 50
2. Compute Jaccard similarity of edge sets
3. Compare to null distribution

Granularity Levels:

· Coarse: Partition by object category (e.g., rigid vs. deformable, large vs. small)
· Fine: Individual object instances
· Global: No partition

F.2.3 Persistent Homology

1. Construct Vietoris-Rips filtration on each representation
2. Compute persistence diagrams for H_0, H_1, H_2
3. Calculate bottleneck distances
4. Compare to null distribution

Computational Considerations:

· Vietoris-Rips filtration is computationally expensive for large point clouds
· Use subsampling (e.g., 500–1000 points) for computational feasibility
· Compare persistence diagrams using bottleneck distance with null calibration

F.2.4 Relational Profile Extraction

1. For each representation, compute the relational profile using the Yoneda embedding
2. Define the cost function as the negative exponentiated distance: c(x, y) = \exp(-d(x, y) / \tau)
3. Compute relational profile equivalence between models
4. Compare to null distribution

F.3 Phase 3: Causal Intervention Testing

F.3.1 Intervention Protocol

1. Identify key environmental variables (e.g., object mass, initial velocity)
2. Perform do-interventions on simulator
3. Measure representation responses
4. Test commutativity diagrams

Specific Interventions:

· Change object mass: \text{do}(\text{mass} = m')
· Change initial velocity: \text{do}(\text{velocity} = v')
· Remove object: \text{do}(\text{object} = \emptyset)

Commutativity Test:

For each intervention, measure:

\epsilon_i = \|\phi_i(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, a)) - \mathcal{T}_{R_i}(\phi_i(S), a)\|

F.3.2 Zero-Shot Transfer Testing

1. Train linear probes on R_i to predict environmental variables
2. Apply probes to R_j
3. Measure transfer accuracy
4. Compare to within-model accuracy

Transfer Score:

\text{Transfer}(i \to j) = \frac{\text{Accuracy}(\text{probe}_i \circ R_j)}{\text{Accuracy}(\text{probe}_i \circ R_i)}

Interpretation:

· Transfer score close to 1: representations are functionally equivalent
· Transfer score close to chance: representations are functionally incompatible

F.3.3 Functional Correspondence Check

For each pair of models, compare their predictions on held-out examples:

\text{FuncCorr}(i, j) = \mathbb{E}_x[\mathbf{1}[f_i(x) = f_j(x)]]

where f_i and f_j are the decision functions of models i and j.

F.4 Phase 4: Scale-Robustness Testing

F.4.1 Protocol

1. Evaluate cross-modal and cross-model alignment at multiple dataset scales
2. Scales: 1K, 10K, 100K, 1M+ samples
3. For each scale, compute calibrated similarity metrics
4. Test whether coarse-grained convergence persists while fine-grained convergence degrades

F.4.2 Statistical Analysis

For each granularity level and scale, test the hypothesis that convergence is significantly above null:

H_0: \text{NS}_k = \text{Null NS}_k

H_1: \text{NS}_k > \text{Null NS}_k

Use permutation tests with Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple comparisons.

F.4.3 Many-to-Many Evaluation

1. For cross-modal pairs, generate multiple valid descriptions for each input
2. Compute many-to-many alignment scores (Section E.7)
3. Compare to one-to-one alignment scores
4. Test whether many-to-many alignment degrades as predicted

F.5 Phase 5: Statistics-Structure Dissociation

F.5.1 Condition 1: Natural Data

· Models trained on data with both environmental structure and statistical regularities
· Baseline condition

F.5.2 Condition 2: Preserved Statistics, Scrambled Structure

· Construct data with preserved statistical symmetries (e.g., power-law distributions, hierarchical structure) but no underlying physical structure
· Example: Generate random graphs with the same degree distribution as physical systems
· If convergence persists here, statistics are the driver

F.5.3 Condition 3: Preserved Structure, Altered Statistics

· Construct data with the same underlying physical laws but different sampling distribution
· Example: Oversample rare configurations, undersample common ones
· If convergence persists here, structure is the driver

F.5.4 Comparison

Compute the dissociation coefficient:

\delta = \kappa_{\text{structure}} - \kappa_{\text{statistics}}

Prediction (CRH): \delta > 0

Alternative (Statistics-Driven): \delta < 0

F.6 Phase 6: Biological Analogue Testing

F.6.1 Protocol

1. Obtain human visual cortex representations (e.g., fMRI data)
2. Train artificial models on natural visual data
3. Test alignability via geometric transformations
4. Compare to human-human alignment baseline

F.6.2 Alignment Method

Use unsupervised alignment via orthogonal Procrustes:

\min_{Q: Q^T Q = I} \|R_{\text{artificial}} Q - R_{\text{human}}\|_F^2

F.6.3 Evaluation

Compare artificial-human alignment to human-human alignment:

\text{AlignRatio} = \frac{\text{Align}(R_{\text{artificial}}, R_{\text{human}})}{\text{Align}(R_{\text{human}_1}, R_{\text{human}_2})}

Prediction (CRH): AlignRatio approaches 1 as model capability increases.

F.7 Statistical Power Analysis

F.7.1 Sample Size Calculation

For detecting convergence rate \alpha with power 1-\beta = 0.8 and significance \alpha_s = 0.05:

N = \left( \frac{z_{1-\alpha_s/2} + z_{1-\beta}}{\delta} \right)^2

where \delta is the expected effect size.

Effect Size Definition:

For the Comparative Convergence Coefficient \kappa, the effect size is:

\delta = \frac{\kappa}{\sigma_\kappa}

where \sigma_\kappa is the standard deviation of \kappa across model pairs.

F.7.2 Multiple Comparison Correction

For m model pairs and k metrics:

\alpha_{\text{corrected}} = \frac{\alpha}{m \cdot k}

For Benjamini-Hochberg:

1. Sort p-values: p_{(1)} \leq p_{(2)} \leq \cdots \leq p_{(m \cdot k)}
2. Find largest j such that p_{(j)} \leq \frac{j}{m \cdot k} \alpha
3. Reject all hypotheses with p \leq p_{(j)}

F.7.3 Sensitivity Analysis

For each experimental condition, report:

· Minimum detectable effect size (MDES) at power 0.8
· Achieved power for the observed effect size
· Confidence intervals for \kappa and \delta

