Appendix I: Proof of the Comparative Convergence Coefficient Theorem

I.1 Statement

Theorem I.1 (Comparative Convergence Coefficient). Let \Gamma(C) be the calibrated, null-adjusted population-level convergence at capability level C. Define:

\kappa(C_1, C_2) = \frac{\Gamma(C_2) - \Gamma(C_1)}{C_2 - C_1}

for C_1 < C_2. Then:

1. If \Gamma(C) is strictly increasing in C, then \kappa > 0 for all C_1 < C_2.
2. If \Gamma(C) is constant in C, then \kappa = 0 for all C_1 < C_2.
3. If \Gamma(C) is strictly decreasing in C, then \kappa < 0 for all C_1 < C_2.

Proof. This follows directly from the definition of \kappa as the slope of the secant line through (C_1, \Gamma(C_1)) and (C_2, \Gamma(C_2)). A strictly increasing function has positive secant slopes; a constant function has zero secant slopes; a strictly decreasing function has negative secant slopes. \square

I.2 Discrimination Between Hypotheses

Theorem I.2 (Hypothesis Discrimination). The following hypotheses make the following predictions about \kappa:

Hypothesis Prediction for \kappa Prediction for \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}
CRH (Mechanism 2) \kappa > 0 \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} > 0
Architectural Priors (Mechanism 1) \kappa \approx 0 \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} \approx 0
Task Geometry Contravariance \kappa > 0 \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} \approx 0
Indra Training-Free \kappa \approx 0 \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} \approx 0

Proof. Each hypothesis makes distinct predictions because each posits a different mechanism:

1. CRH: Environmental structure constrains representations as capability increases. This predicts \kappa > 0 because higher capability models are more constrained. In pseudo-physics environments, the "structure" is arbitrary, so convergence should be weaker: \kappa_{\text{real}} > \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}.
2. Architectural Priors: Convergence is determined by shared inductive biases at initialization. Capability does not affect convergence: \kappa = 0. This is true for both real and pseudo-physics environments.
3. Contravariance: Convergence is driven by optimization geometry, which is identical for real and pseudo-physics environments of the same computational complexity: \kappa_{\text{real}} = \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}.
4. Indra: The Yoneda embedding is canonical and capability-independent: \kappa = 0.

\square

I.3 Statistical Test

Theorem I.3 (Hypothesis Test for \kappa). Under the null hypothesis \kappa = 0, the test statistic:

t = \frac{\hat{\kappa}}{\hat{\sigma}_\kappa}

follows a t-distribution with m - 2 degrees of freedom, where m is the number of capability levels at which convergence is measured.

Proof. \hat{\kappa} is the slope of a linear regression of \Gamma on C. Under the null hypothesis of zero slope, the standardized slope follows a t-distribution. \square

Power Analysis:

The power to detect \kappa > 0 depends on:

· The true value of \kappa
· The number of capability levels m
· The spread of capability levels
· The noise in convergence measurements

For \kappa = 0.1 (a moderate effect), m = 5 capability levels, and noise \sigma = 0.05, the power exceeds 0.8 for a one-sided test at \alpha = 0.05.

I.4 Generalization to Multiple Models

Theorem I.4 (Population-Level Convergence). For n independently trained models, the population-level convergence is:

\Gamma_{\text{population}} = \frac{2}{n(n-1)} \sum_{i<j} \Gamma_{ij}

where \Gamma_{ij} is the pairwise convergence between models i and j.

The Comparative Convergence Coefficient for the population is:

\kappa_{\text{population}} = \frac{\Gamma_{\text{population}}(C_2) - \Gamma_{\text{population}}(C_1)}{C_2 - C_1}

Properties:

1. \kappa_{\text{population}} is an unbiased estimator of the population-level convergence rate.
2. The variance of \kappa_{\text{population}} decreases as O(1/n^2) as the number of models increases.

Proof. (1) follows from linearity of expectation. (2) follows from the fact that \Gamma_{\text{population}} is an average of n(n-1)/2 pairwise terms, and the variance of an average decreases as O(1/m) where m is the number of terms. \square

