Appendix J: Mathematical Details of the Relational Profile Framework

J.1 Enriched Category Theory

J.1.1 Monoidal Categories

Definition. A monoidal category (\mathcal{V}, \otimes, I) consists of:

· A category \mathcal{V}
· A bifunctor \otimes: \mathcal{V} \times \mathcal{V} \to \mathcal{V} (tensor product)
· A unit object I \in \mathcal{V}
· Natural isomorphisms satisfying coherence conditions (associativity, left and right unit)

Examples:

· (\mathbf{Set}, \times, \{*\}): sets with Cartesian product
· (\mathbf{Met}, \times, \{*\}): metric spaces with product metric
· ([0, \infty], +, 0): non-negative real numbers with addition (Lawvere metric spaces)

J.1.2 Enriched Categories

Definition. A category \mathcal{C} enriched over \mathcal{V} consists of:

· A set of objects \text{Ob}(\mathcal{C})
· For each pair of objects x, y, a hom-object \mathcal{C}(x, y) \in \mathcal{V}
· Composition morphisms: \mathcal{C}(y, z) \otimes \mathcal{C}(x, y) \to \mathcal{C}(x, z)
· Identity morphisms: I \to \mathcal{C}(x, x)
· Satisfying associativity and identity axioms

J.1.3 The Enriched Yoneda Embedding

Definition. For a \mathcal{V}-enriched category \mathcal{C}, the \mathcal{V}-enriched Yoneda embedding is:

Y: \mathcal{C} \to [\mathcal{C}^{\text{op}}, \mathcal{V}]

defined by:

Y(c) = \mathcal{C}(-, c)

Theorem J.1 (Enriched Yoneda Lemma). For any c \in \mathcal{C} and any functor F: \mathcal{C}^{\text{op}} \to \mathcal{V}:

[\mathcal{C}^{\text{op}}, \mathcal{V}](Y(c), F) \cong F(c)

naturally in c and F.

Proof. See Kelly (1982), Chapter 1. \square

Corollary J.1.1 (Completeness). The Yoneda embedding is full and faithful. That is, for any c, d \in \mathcal{C}:

[\mathcal{C}^{\text{op}}, \mathcal{V}](Y(c), Y(d)) \cong \mathcal{C}(c, d)

J.2 Relational Profiles in Metric Spaces

J.2.1 The Environmental Cost Category

Definition. Let (\mathcal{S}, d_{\mathcal{S}}) be a metric space of environmental states. The environmental cost category \mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{E}} is the category enriched over ([0, \infty], +, 0) with:

· Objects: Environmental states S \in \mathcal{S}
· Hom-objects: \mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{E}}(S, T) = d_{\mathcal{S}}(S, T)

Note: This is a Lawvere metric space, where composition is the triangle inequality and identity is d(S, S) = 0.

J.2.2 Relational Profiles of Environmental States

Definition. The relational profile of environmental state S is:

\Phi(S): \mathcal{S} \to [0, \infty], \quad T \mapsto d_{\mathcal{S}}(S, T)

This assigns to each state T the distance from S to T.

Theorem J.2 (Completeness of Relational Profiles). Two environmental states S_1, S_2 are isometric if and only if their relational profiles are equivalent:

d(S_1, S_2) = 0 \iff \Phi(S_1) = \Phi(S_2)

Proof. This is the triangle inequality: if \Phi(S_1) = \Phi(S_2), then d(S_1, S_2) = \Phi(S_1)(S_2) = \Phi(S_2)(S_2) = 0. Conversely, if d(S_1, S_2) = 0, then S_1 = S_2 (in a metric space), so \Phi(S_1) = \Phi(S_2). \square

J.2.3 Relational Profiles of Representations

For a learning system i, the representation induces a cost category \mathcal{C}_i with:

· Objects: Representation points z \in \mathcal{M}_i
· Hom-objects: \mathcal{C}_i(z, w) = d_i(z, w)

The relational profile of z in representation i is:

\Phi_i(z): \mathcal{M}_i \to [0, \infty], \quad w \mapsto d_i(z, w)

Definition. The relational profile of environmental state S under model i is:

\Phi_i(S): \mathcal{S} \to [0, \infty], \quad T \mapsto d_i(R_i(S), R_i(T))

This measures how state S relates to all other states in the representation space of model i.

J.3 Relational Profile Convergence

J.3.1 Definition

Definition. Models i and j exhibit relational profile convergence at level \epsilon if:

\sup_{S \in \mathcal{S}} d_{\infty}(\Phi_i(S), \Phi_j(S)) < \epsilon

where d_{\infty} is the sup norm on functions \mathcal{S} \to [0, \infty].

J.3.2 Relationship to Gromov-Wasserstein Convergence

Theorem J.3. If d_{GW}(\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j) < \epsilon, then the relational profiles converge at level 2\epsilon.

Proof. The Gromov-Wasserstein distance bounds the discrepancy between pairwise distance matrices. The relational profile of S is a row of the distance matrix. If the full matrices are within \epsilon, then individual rows are within 2\epsilon. \square

Note: The converse does not hold. Relational profiles can converge even when the full distance matrices do not, since the profiles capture local structure more robustly than global distances.

J.3.3 The Indra Uniqueness Property

Theorem J.4 (Indra Uniqueness). For any cost function c, the relational profile embedding is unique up to isomorphism.

Proof. This follows from the Yoneda lemma: the relational profile functor is fully faithful, so any two relational profiles that are naturally isomorphic correspond to isomorphic objects. \square

Implication: If different models converge to the same relational profiles, they are representationally equivalent in the strongest possible sense: there is no structural information in one that is not in the other.

