k-NN Graph O(n^2 d) O(n k) Feasible for n \leq 10^5
Neighborhood Similarity O(n^2 d + n k) O(n k) Feasible for n \leq 10^5
Wasserstein Distance O(n^3 \log n) O(n^2) Feasible for n \leq 10^4
Gromov-Wasserstein O(n^3) (exact) O(n^2) Infeasible for large n
Entropic GW O(n^2) per iteration O(n^2) Feasible for n \leq 10^4
Persistent Homology O(n^3) (worst case) O(n^2) Feasible for n \leq 10^3
Relational Profile O(n^2 d) O(n^2) Feasible for n \leq 10^4

K.2 Approximations

For large-scale evaluation, the following approximations are recommended:

1. Entropic GW: Add entropy regularization to make the optimization convex and computationally tractable
2. Sliced GW: Project representations to lower dimensions before computing GW
3. Subsampled Persistent Homology: Compute persistence diagrams on random subsamples
4. Approximate Nearest Neighbors: Use locality-sensitive hashing or k-d trees for k-NN computation
5. Randomized SVD: Approximate the Gram matrix for CKA computation

K.3 Recommended Pipeline

For large-scale evaluation:

1. Subsample to n = 10^4 points
2. Use approximate k-NN for neighborhood similarity
3. Use entropic GW for global geometry
4. Use subsampled persistent homology (500–1000 points) for topology
5. Use full dataset for relational profile computation

