Appendix L: Relationship to Existing Theoretical Frameworks

L.1 Information Bottleneck

The Information Bottleneck (IB) framework posits that representations should maximize mutual information with outputs while minimizing mutual information with inputs:

\max_{R} I(R; Y) - \beta I(R; X)

Connection to CRH: IB provides a mechanism for convergence: if different models optimize the same IB objective, they may converge to similar representations. However, IB does not predict convergence to environmental structure per se, only to predictive sufficiency.

L.2 Rate-Distortion Theory

Rate-distortion theory formalizes the trade-off between compression and fidelity:

R(D) = \min_{P(\hat{X}|X): \mathbb{E}[d(X, \hat{X})] \leq D} I(X; \hat{X})

Connection to CRH: The rate-distortion function provides a lower bound on the information that must be retained in any representation achieving a given fidelity. This constrains the set of valid representations, supporting CRH's claim that high capability narrows representational possibilities.

L.3 Causal Representation Learning

Causal representation learning aims to learn representations that capture causal structure:

P(R(X) | \text{do}(R(Y))) \approx P(R(X | \text{do}(Y)))

Connection to CRH: Level III convergence is closely related to causal representation learning. Both require that representations preserve causal relationships. CRH adds the claim that different models converge on the same causal structure.

L.4 Efficient Coding Hypothesis

The efficient coding hypothesis (Barlow) states that sensory systems are optimized to efficiently encode natural statistics:

\min I(R; X) \quad \text{subject to} \quad I(R; Y) \geq I_0

Connection to CRH: Efficient coding predicts convergence on statistical structure, not necessarily on causal or topological structure. CRH makes stronger claims about the type of structure that converges.

L.5 Predictive Coding

Predictive coding (Friston) proposes that the brain implements hierarchical predictive models:

\min \mathbb{E}[-\log P(X | R)]

Connection to CRH: Predictive coding provides a mechanism for convergence: if models minimize prediction error, they may converge to the true generative model. This is consistent with CRH's Mechanism 2.

L.6 The Indra Framework

The Indra Representation Hypothesis formalizes convergence via relational profiles:

R(x) = Y(x) = \mathcal{C}(-, x)

Connection to CRH: Indra provides an alternative formalization of convergence that is capability-independent. CRH and Indra are complementary: CRH explains why convergence increases with capability, while Indra explains the canonical structure that convergent representations approximate.

L.7 Neural Tangent Kernel Theory

NTK theory describes the evolution of neural network outputs during training:

f_t(x) \approx f_0(x) + \sum_{i} (y_i - f_0(x_i)) K(x, x_i)

Connection to CRH: NTK predicts that early-training representations are determined by the kernel, which depends on architecture and initialization. This is consistent with Mechanism 1 (early crystallization). Mechanism 2 requires going beyond the NTK regime, into feature learning.

