Appendix O: Comparative Convergence Coefficient Proof

O.1 Complete Proof of the Discrimination Theorem

Theorem O.1 (Complete Discrimination Theorem). Let \kappa be the Comparative Convergence Coefficient and let \kappa_{\text{real}} and \kappa_{\text{pseudo}} be the coefficients for real and pseudo-physics environments, respectively. The following hypotheses make the following predictions:

Hypothesis \kappa \kappa_{\text{real}} - \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}
CRH (Mechanism 2) > 0 > 0
Architectural Priors (Mechanism 1) = 0 = 0
Task Geometry Contravariance > 0 = 0
Indra Training-Free = 0 = 0
Statistics-Driven Convergence > 0 Depends on statistics similarity

Proof.

(1) CRH: Environmental structure constrains representations as capability increases. This predicts \kappa > 0 because higher capability models are more constrained by environmental structure. In pseudo-physics environments, the "structure" is arbitrary (human-designed), so convergence should be weaker: \kappa_{\text{real}} > \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}.

(2) Architectural Priors: Convergence is determined by shared inductive biases at initialization. Capability does not affect convergence: \kappa = 0. This is true for both real and pseudo-physics environments.

(3) Contravariance: Convergence is driven by optimization geometry, which is identical for real and pseudo-physics environments of the same computational complexity: \kappa_{\text{real}} = \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}.

(4) Indra: The Yoneda embedding is canonical and capability-independent: \kappa = 0.

(5) Statistics-Driven: Convergence is driven by data statistics. If real and pseudo-physics environments have similar statistics, \kappa_{\text{real}} = \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}. If they differ, \kappa_{\text{real}} \neq \kappa_{\text{pseudo}}. This is distinguished from CRH by the statistics-structure dissociation test: \delta (defined in Section E.5.2) is negative for statistics-driven convergence and positive for CRH.

\square

O.2 Statistical Power Analysis

Theorem O.2 (Required Sample Size). To detect \kappa > 0 with power 1-\beta and significance \alpha, the required number of capability levels m satisfies:

m \geq \left( \frac{z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta}}{\kappa / \sigma} \right)^2 + 2

where \sigma is the standard deviation of convergence measurements and z_q is the q-quantile of the standard normal distribution.

Proof. The test statistic is t = \hat{\kappa} / \hat{\sigma}_\kappa, which follows a non-central t-distribution with non-centrality parameter \lambda = \kappa / \sigma_\kappa under the alternative. The power is:

1-\beta = P(t > t_{1-\alpha, m-2} | \lambda)

For large m, the non-central t-distribution approximates a normal distribution with mean \lambda and unit variance. Thus:

z_{1-\beta} \approx \lambda - z_{1-\alpha}

Solving for m (through \sigma_\kappa = \sigma / \sqrt{\sum (C_i - \bar{C})^2}):

m \geq \left( \frac{z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta}}{\kappa / \sigma} \right)^2 + 2

\square

Example: For \kappa = 0.1, \sigma = 0.05, \alpha = 0.05, \beta = 0.2:

m \geq \left( \frac{1.645 + 0.842}{0.1 / 0.05} \right)^2 + 2 = \left( \frac{2.487}{2} \right)^2 + 2 \approx 3.55

So m = 4 capability levels suffice. With m = 5, the power exceeds 0.9.

O.3 Generalization to Population-Level Convergence

Theorem O.3 (Population-Level \kappa). For n models, the population-level convergence is:

\Gamma_{\text{pop}}(C) = \frac{2}{n(n-1)} \sum_{i<j} \Gamma_{ij}(C)

The population-level \kappa is:

\kappa_{\text{pop}} = \frac{\Gamma_{\text{pop}}(C_2) - \Gamma_{\text{pop}}(C_1)}{C_2 - C_1}

Properties:

1. \mathbb{E}[\kappa_{\text{pop}}] = \mathbb{E}[\kappa_{ij}] (unbiased)
2. \text{Var}(\kappa_{\text{pop}}) = \frac{2}{n(n-1)} \text{Var}(\kappa_{ij}) + O(n^{-2})

Proof. (1) follows from linearity of expectation. (2) follows from the variance of the mean of pairwise terms. \square

Implication: Increasing the number of models n reduces the variance of \kappa_{\text{pop}}, making it easier to detect convergence. This is the statistical justification for training many models rather than just two.

